      "claim": "Atmospheric pressure decreases with altitude, so the pressure at the mountain top is lower than at the valley.",
      "evidence": "Standard atmospheric physics: pressure ~ e^{-h/H}.",
      "weight": 0.2,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "While the bottle was open on the mountain top, the air inside the bottle was at the same low pressure as the outside atmosphere.",
      "evidence": "Open container equilibrates its internal pressure with external pressure.",
      "weight": 0.2,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "After the water was drunk, the bottle was sealed, trapping the low‑pressure air inside.",
      "evidence": "The cover was put back on while the bottle was empty.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "When the man descended to the valley, the external atmospheric pressure increased to a higher value.",
      "evidence": "From step 1, pressure is higher at lower altitude.",
      "weight": 0.2,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "The higher external pressure compresses the sealed low‑pressure bottle, causing it to collapse.",
      "evidence": "Pressure difference across a flexible container produces net force; if external > internal, the container collapses.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [3, 4]
    }
  ],
  "answer": "D",
  "confidence": 0.99,
  "considered": [
    {
      "answer": "A",
      "reason_rejected": "Temperature differences do not create a net pressure sufficient to crush the bottle."
    },
